


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: torrente
@title: From DIDONE to ChordIA: A Cumulative Research Ecosystem in Digital Musicology
@presenter: torrente
@abstract: 

This paper presents an overview of several interconnected
research initiatives that together outline a shared
philosophy for digital musicology: one grounded in the
systematic construction of large digital corpora and in the
reuse of tools and computational methodologies across
projects. Within this common framework, some initiatives
form a cumulative chain in which each project extends the
resources of the previous one, while others run in
parallel, applying the same data-driven ethos to different
repertories and methods—such as ReSound, which
reconstructs the lost sonority of Old Hispanic chant
through phylogenetic and bioinformatic tools, or LexiMus,
which mobilises a vast textual corpus to build a
specialised music dictionary using modern lexicographic
techniques.

The cumulative line originates with the ERC-funded DIDONE
project, which built a corpus of some 3,000 digitised arias
from eighteenth-century <i>opera seria</i> on Metastasio’s most
popular librettos, together with the musif
feature-extraction library, to investigate how composers
musically encoded emotions. Building directly on this
resource, DEePMusic developed and applied the technology
that allows the DIDONE arias database to be visualised in
real time on any device, turning a research corpus into a
dynamic, openly accessible interface—effectively a
digital publication of a vast and unique music collection.

Repertorium, a Horizon Europe project combining musicology
and sound processing, drew on the same tradition of digital
editions: our contribution supplied digital editions of
eighteenth-century arias as well as images of the original
music manuscripts to train an AI system in a process of
reverse engineering—learning to generate digital editions
automatically from the manuscript sources through optical
music recognition (OMR).

Finally, ChordIA takes the 3,000 DIDONE arias as its point
of departure, now expanded with corpora from earlier
repertories predating 1720, in order to model harmony
itself through an architecture of AI agents and symbolic
models for the deep harmonic analysis of the period when
tonality was still being forged.


@@END: PRESENTATION



